This is an excellent interview topic. Interviewers often ask tricky questions on Varargs (...) and method overloading.

What is Varargs?

Varargs (Variable Arguments) allow a method to accept 0 or more arguments of the same type.

Syntax

dataType... variableName

Example

class Test {

    static void add(int... numbers) {

        for (int n : numbers) {
            System.out.print(n + " ");
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        add();
        add(10);
        add(10,20,30);
        add(10,20,30,40,50);
    }
}

Output

10
10 20 30
10 20 30 40 50
Internally how does Varargs work?

The compiler converts varargs into an array.

You write

add(10,20,30);

Compiler converts it to

add(new int[]{10,20,30});

That's why inside the method you can use it like an array.

static void add(int... arr){

    System.out.println(arr.length);
}
Can we pass an array?

Yes.

int arr[] = {10,20,30};

add(arr);

Perfectly valid.

Can varargs accept zero arguments?

Yes.

add();

Inside

numbers.length == 0
Can we have normal parameters before varargs?

Yes.

static void display(String name, int... marks){

}

Call

display("Harshal",90,95,98);
Can we have parameters after varargs?

❌ No

static void display(int... marks,String name)

Compile-time Error

Reason:

The compiler won't know where the varargs end and the next parameter begins.

Can we have two varargs?

❌ No

void test(int... a,String... b)

Compile-time Error.

Only one varargs parameter is allowed.

Varargs must always be last

✔ Correct

void display(int a,int b,int... c)

❌ Wrong

void display(int... a,int b)
Is varargs mandatory?

No.

You can call

display();

or

display(10);

or

display(10,20,30);
Overloading with Varargs

This is where interviewers ask tricky questions.

Case 1
void show(int a){

    System.out.println("int");
}

void show(int... a){

    System.out.println("varargs");
}

Call

show(10);

Output

int

Why?

Exact match is always preferred over varargs.

Case 2
void show(Integer a){

    System.out.println("Integer");
}

void show(int... a){

    System.out.println("varargs");
}

Call

show(10);

Output

Integer

Reason

10

↓

Autoboxing

↓

Integer

Compiler prefers autoboxing over varargs.

Priority

Exact Match

↓

Widening

↓

Autoboxing

↓

Varargs
Case 3
void show(long a){

    System.out.println("long");
}

void show(int... a){

    System.out.println("varargs");
}

Call

show(10);

Output

long

Reason

Widening (int → long) is preferred over varargs.

Case 4
void show(int... a){

    System.out.println("Varargs");
}

void show(int b,int c){

    System.out.println("Two Int");
}

Call

show(10,20);

Output

Two Int

Exact match wins.

Case 5
void show(int... a){

    System.out.println("int");
}

void show(String... a){

    System.out.println("String");
}

Call

show();

Compile-time Error

Reference to show() is ambiguous

Reason

The compiler doesn't know whether the empty call should match int... or String....

Case 6
void show(Object... a){

    System.out.println("Object");
}

void show(String... a){

    System.out.println("String");
}

Call

show("Harshal");

Output

String

Reason

String is more specific than Object.

Can we overload only by varargs?

Example

void show(int... a){

}

and

void show(int[] a){

}

❌ Compile-time Error

Reason

Varargs is internally an array.

Compiler sees both as

show(int[])

Duplicate method.

Limitations of Varargs
1. Only one varargs parameter
void test(int... a,String... b)

❌

2. Must be the last parameter
void test(int... a,String name)

❌

3. Can make method calls ambiguous
show();

when

show(int...)
show(String...)

Both match.

4. Slight overhead

Every varargs call creates (or uses) an array.

show(1,2,3);

Compiler creates

new int[]{1,2,3}

This has a small performance cost compared to a fixed-parameter method.

Interview Priority Rule (Very Important)

When multiple overloaded methods are available, Java chooses them in this order:

Priority	Method Type
1	Exact Match
2	Primitive Widening
3	Autoboxing
4	Varargs

Example:

void show(int a){
    System.out.println("int");
}

void show(long a){
    System.out.println("long");
}

void show(Integer a){
    System.out.println("Integer");
}

void show(int... a){
    System.out.println("varargs");
}

public static void main(String[] args) {
    show(10);
}

Output:

int